Hi,

When running bonnie++ (version 1.93c) against a NFS server running the
latest 2.5.* kernels (I've tried 2.5.70, 2.5.73-bk3, and 2.5.73-mm1),
bonnie++ is dying in the rewriting stage with a file larger than 4 GB
(ram size set greater than 2048 MB with the -r flag) with the error:

  Can't write block.: Invalid argument
  Bonnie: drastic I/O error (rewrite(2)): Invalid argument

The client machine in this case is running RedHat's 2.4.18-27.7 kernel. 
When the server is running 2.4.21, bonnie++ finishes without error. 
Running bonnie++ directly on the server (not through NFS) when the
server is running any of the above 2.5 kernels works fine.  A bonnie++
ram setting of 2048 MB (file size 4096 MB) is the largest I've seen
successful; as soon as the size is increased to 2049 MB (file size 4098
MB), the errors start occuring.

The export on the server is an ext3 filesystem on top of software
RAID0.  The client is mounting it as:

  rw,v3,rsize=32768,wsize=32768,hard,udp,lock,addr=primer

A strace when bonnie++ fails looks like:

read(3, "AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA"..., 8192) = 8192
_llseek(3, -8192, [4295548928], SEEK_CUR) = 0
write(3, "AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA"..., 8192) = 8192
gettimeofday({1056551978, 576269}, NULL) = 0
gettimeofday({1056551978, 576323}, NULL) = 0
read(3, "AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA"..., 8192) = 8192
_llseek(3, -8192, [4295557120], SEEK_CUR) = 0
write(3, "AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA"..., 8192) = -1 EINVAL
(Invalid argument)
write(2, "Can\'t write block.: Invalid argu"..., 37Can't write block.:
Invalid argument
) = 37
write(2, "Bonnie: drastic I/O error (re wr"..., 58Bonnie: drastic I/O
error (re write(2)): Invalid argument
) = 58

I've attached the .config for the server.  Any idea what may be going
wrong?  I've seen similar problems with iozone as well.

Thanks,

--
Jason Holmes
